course: dictionary
lesson: 2147
title: Fossil poo, the coprolite
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A coprolite is fossilised poo. It is the droppings of an animal that lived long ago, and it has slowly turned to stone. Context helps you work out the word: «Coprolites are fossilised droppings that show what ancient animals ate.» The word "fossilised" is your clue, because fossils are things from long ago that have turned to stone.
explain: Scientists call coprolites trace fossils. A trace fossil shows what an animal did or ate, not what its body looked like. Inside a coprolite, scientists can find bits of bone, fish scales or plants, so the poo tells a story about the animal's dinner.
explain: Try the swap check. «One T. rex coprolite, as big as a loaf of bread, was full of crushed bone from a plant-eater.» If you put in "one T. rex fossil poo", the sentence still makes sense, so you know your meaning fits.
fact: A coprolite is fossil poo that has slowly turned to stone.
fact: Coprolites are trace fossils: they show what ancient animals ate.
fact: Scientists can find bits of bone, fish scales or plants inside a coprolite.
fact: «William Buckland» recognised coprolites in the 1820s, and «Mary Anning» noticed them inside ichthyosaur skeletons.
fact: One T. rex coprolite was as big as a loaf of bread and full of crushed bone from a plant-eater.
exercise: What is a coprolite? | A stone shaped like a bone | *Fossilised poo from an animal that lived long ago | A dinosaur egg that has hatched | Mud dried out by the sun
exercise: «One T. rex coprolite was full of crushed bone from a plant-eater.» What can this coprolite show scientists? | How fast the dinosaur could run | *What the animal ate | What colour its skin was | When the dinosaur was born
exercise: What is a trace fossil? | A rock with no fossils in it | A fossil of a plant leaf only | *A fossil that shows what an animal did or ate | Something that is still alive
test: «Mary Anning noticed coprolites inside ichthyosaur skeletons.» Where did she notice them? | Inside a volcano | On a hot beach | A museum shop | *Inside ichthyosaur skeletons
test: Why might scientists study a coprolite? | To make a fossil from new poo | To count how many dinosaurs lived | *To find out what an ancient animal ate, using the bits inside it, such as bone or scales | To find out what an ancient animal ate
exam: Using the word «coprolite», explain in your own words what a coprolite is and what it can tell scientists about ancient animals. | A stone that was used to make tools | *A fossilised poo that shows what an ancient animal ate | A dinosaur bone with no meat on it | A rock that floats in water
